<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bayonne</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bayonne is a city in the French Basque Country, where the Nive joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adour">Adour</a> near the Atlantic. Under English rule from 1152 to 1451 it was a busy port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a>, and its fortifications were later rebuilt by Vauban. In 1808 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> forced the Spanish kings Charles IV and Ferdinand VII to abdicate there, and the town is said to have given its name to the bayonet.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bayonne.jpg" alt="Bayonne" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daniel VILLAFRUELA · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bayonne-Centre_historique-20130811.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
